{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793716594,"nonce":"nOnmcGe0p5aQqcpFP2CrEBnJu0E=","title":"zylua: remove nil guards and preserve exact receivers through verified self returns","message":"Follow-up to 9344e9fe03a5460d8a484af7846826727646177095029e7ccb76a84749bf0a04 and the open cold-path issue e8fe1d3. Fresh constructor locals already prove a non-nil table, but which_call still emits a nil guard and its inline error path. Method-call chains discard the receiver proof even when every normal return is the unchanged first parameter.\n\nUse the existing freshness proof to omit only redundant nil guards. Summarize methods whose first result on every normal return is their unchanged first parameter, then retain exact receiver identity only when the resolved method targets all satisfy that summary. Rebinding, nil/other returns, shadowed self, fallthrough, dynamic lookup, field calls with an unrelated first argument and error propagation must remain correct. Prove code reduction structurally, pin adversarial fixtures against Lua 5.4, run the full release Lua suite and clippy, then measure interleaved frozen LLVM-enabled binaries on the NUC. Field-initialization facts are a separate follow-up; exact class does not establish field presence.","files":null}]}